<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latvians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Letts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Latvians are a Baltic people native to Latvia, speaking Latvian, one of the two surviving Baltic languages. They formed from the merging of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a>, Selonians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> and Curonians with assimilated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>, under centuries of German, Polish, Swedish and Russian rule. A national awakening in the 19th century led to the founding of the independent Republic of Latvia in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latvians.jpg" alt="Latvians" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Latvian_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Interwar Latvia had about two million people, roughly three-quarters of them ethnic <b>Latvians</b>, with substantial Russian, Jewish, German, Polish and Belarusian minorities, and a religious divide between Lutheran Courland, Livonia and Zemgale and Catholic Latgale.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A small group of Curonian freemen, the 'Curonian kings', kept a special status into modern times, and the people merged into the <b>Latvians</b> and Lithuanians.</blockquote><a href="/state/curonians">Curonians</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Latvians</b>, the great majority of the population, were excluded from its councils and rejected it.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia-1918">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia (1918)</a></li><li><blockquote>Riga became a major industrial centre, producing radios, railway carriages and the RAF minibuses, which drew large numbers of Russian-speaking workers, so that <b>Latvians</b> fell to barely half the population.</blockquote><a href="/state/latvian-soviet-socialist-republic">Latvian Soviet Socialist Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The area became part of the Archbishopric of Riga and Terra Mariana, and the Livonian population merged gradually into the <b>Latvians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/metsepole">Metsepole</a></li><li><blockquote>It also sponsored newspapers, schools and surveys among Lithuanians, <b>Latvians</b>, Poles, Jews and Belarusians, seeing the region as a German colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/ober-ost">Ober Ost</a></li></ul></section>
